<h2><span class="n">Part 1</span> The Live Desk — the week the router earned its keep</h2>

<p class="lead">Garrath — the 6-gate paper tournament made <strong>+$625</strong> across <strong>206 trades</strong> this week (Mon 27th through Fri 31st, on the Paris session-day boundary the desk keeps its books on). That headline hides a wild ride: <strong>one best-ever day (+$1,172 on Thursday)</strong>, one clean two-way trend day (+$638 Wednesday), a near-scratch Tuesday, and two red days that bookended the week — a choppy Monday (−$227) and an ugly, whippy Friday (−$905). The money came from exactly two places: the gates that <em>ride</em> a real move (grind and abs_veto on the trend legs) and the gate that <em>fades</em> a tired one (exhaustion_short). Everything else either scratched or bled, and the router spent the week benching the bleeders. Below is the whole thing, every number computed off the tournament trades, honest about the fact that it is one week of in-sample paper.</p>

<div class="callout"><div class="ct"><strong>Read this first — what SURVIVED the week.</strong> If you strip away the noise, one gate stood up on almost every kind of day: <strong>exhaustion_short was green on 4 of the 5 sessions</strong> (+$14, +$90, +$95, +$217, and only −$100 on Friday's violence), 54% of its trades won, +$316 for the week. It is the steadiest earner on the desk. The flashier numbers — grind's +$704 Thursday, abs_veto_short's +$570 Wednesday — are real but they are <em>day-type bets</em>: they printed on the one or two days the tape actually trended and gave money back the rest of the time. Lead with the steady one; treat the big ones as conditional.</div></div>

<div class="card">
<h3>The week, day by day</h3>
<p>Every day scored on the desk's own Paris session boundary, so these match the numbers in your ledger (Thursday's +$1,172 is the best single day the desk has ever booked). The "character" column is the router's live regime read, not a number I invented — it's what actually drove the bench/arm calls.</p>
