<tr><td class="ln">Tue 07-28</td><td class="num">−54.0</td><td class="num">23</td><td>Quiet, low-vol chop. Near-scratch — the shorts (abs_veto +$68, exhaustion +$90) roughly paid for the longs' churn.</td></tr>
<tr class="row-hl"><td class="ln">Wed 07-29</td><td class="num">+638.5</td><td class="num">17</td><td>The clean trend day — a strong down-leg then a strong up-leg, and the desk caught BOTH sides. Only 17 trades: when it's real, you don't need many.</td></tr>
<tr class="row-hl"><td class="ln">Thu 07-30</td><td class="num">+1,172.0</td><td class="num">91</td><td>Best day ever. An up-bias that topped out and spent the whole US session whipping in a 1,205-point range. 91 trades — the router fought this one all day (more below).</td></tr>
<tr class="row-bad"><td class="ln">Fri 07-31</td><td class="num">−905.0</td><td class="num">35</td><td>Worst day. A US-open up-thrust that fully reversed into an ER-0.01 chop; an "enable all at the open" call handed the churners the reversal.</td></tr>
<tr><td class="ln"><strong>Week</strong></td><td class="num"><strong>+625.0</strong></td><td class="num"><strong>206</strong></td><td>39% of trades won (80 of 206). Two green days paid for three red/scratch ones.</td></tr>
</tbody>
</table>
<p>The shape of that week is the whole story: the desk does not need to win most days, it needs to be <em>on the right gates</em> on the two days that trend and <em>out of the way</em> on the days that don't. Thursday and Wednesday together made +$1,811; the other three days gave −$1,186 of it back. Net +$625.</p>
</div>

