<p>Two things jump out. First, <strong>grind is a low-win, big-winner machine</strong> — it only won 32% of its trades but its average winner (+$125) is more than double its average loser (−$54); it loses small a lot and wins big occasionally, which is exactly what a trend-rider should look like. Second, <strong>rgv_short barely fired at all</strong> (3 trades) — the router had it benched for almost the whole week because it kept trying to short an up-biased tape, and the 3 that slipped through all stopped. That's not a broken gate, that's the router correctly keeping it in the dugout.</p>
</div>

<div class="card">
<h3>Where the money actually came from — the exit mix</h3>
<p>This is the most honest picture of the week. The stops are enormous in aggregate — the desk paid over six thousand dollars in stop-outs — but that's the <em>price of admission</em>: you take a lot of small stops to be present for the few big tails. The green all comes from three exits: the chandelier tails, the fixed targets, and the trades you claimed by hand.</p>
<table>
<thead><tr><th>Exit reason</th><th class="num">Count</th><th class="num">Net $</th><th>What it is</th></tr></thead>
<tbody>
<tr class="row-hl"><td class="ln">CHANDELIER</td><td class="num">32</td><td class="num">+3,065.0</td><td>The trailing-tail exit — the big runners. The single biggest profit bucket.</td></tr>
<tr class="row-hl"><td class="ln">TARGET</td><td class="num">33</td><td class="num">+2,647.5</td><td>Fixed profit target hit.</td></tr>
<tr class="row-hl"><td class="ln">MANUAL_CLAIM</td><td class="num">12</td><td class="num">+1,223.0</td><td>You claimed the profit by hand (a lot of the exhaustion fades).</td></tr>
<tr><td class="ln">GIVEBACK</td><td class="num">4</td><td class="num">+82.0</td><td>Give-back trail banked a small green.</td></tr>
<tr class="row-bad"><td class="ln">STOP</td><td class="num">119</td><td class="num">−6,119.0</td><td>Normal stop-outs — the cost of being present.</td></tr>
<tr class="row-bad"><td class="ln">STOP_UNFILLED</td><td class="num">6</td><td class="num">−273.5</td><td>The software-stop failures, caught by the guard (see below).</td></tr>
</tbody>
</table>
<p>The lesson baked in here: <strong>the tail is the edge.</strong> When you split each gate into its scalp lot (Lot A) and its chandelier tail lot (Lot B), the tail carries nearly all the money — grind's tail made +$302 while its scalp made +$31; abs_veto_long's tail +$322 vs its scalp +$5; abs_veto_short's tail +$380 vs +$52. The scalp lots roughly break even and lock in certainty; the tail lots are where the week was won.</p>
</div>

