<h2><span class="n">Case 1</span> Thursday — the best day ever, and the router in the ring</h2>

<p class="lead">Thursday (+$1,172) is the week's headline and its best case study, because it was <em>not</em> a clean trend — it was an up-biased tape that topped out around lunchtime and then whipped violently for hours, and the desk still booked its best day ever by staying on the right gates through a dozen regime flips. This is the day to study when you want to know what the router is actually for.</p>

<div class="card">
<h3>How Thursday was won</h3>
<p>The morning trended up and grind rode it — grind_long made <strong>+$307 across 37 trades</strong> on the day (32 of them UTC-Thursday), catching the up-legs with its chandelier tail. Then around 13:50 UTC the buy-climax printed (aggressor delta peaked, then buy intensity collapsed to a third), the tape rolled over, and the router flipped the whole book: benched the momentum longs and armed the short-side reversion faders. The result was the cleanest offensive sequence of the week — <strong>exhaustion_short banked four fade-claims in a row for +$372.5</strong> (+$117, +$107.5, +$109.5, +$38.5) fading the roll-over. It then gave −$155 back on two later fires when the chop tried to resume up, netting +$217 on the day — but that four-in-a-row is the exhaustion edge on full display: judge this gate on its live fades, not its fixed-target shadow.</p>
<table>
<thead><tr><th>Thursday's engine</th><th class="num">Net $</th><th class="num">N</th><th>Note</th></tr></thead>
<tbody>
<tr class="row-hl"><td class="ln">grind_long (up-legs)</td><td class="num">+704</td><td class="num">46</td><td>Rode the morning trend + the afternoon resumptions with the chandelier tail.</td></tr>
<tr class="row-hl"><td class="ln">abs_veto_long</td><td class="num">+242</td><td class="num">35</td><td>The veto-protected long — caught the confirmed breaks, sat out the fakes.</td></tr>
<tr class="row-hl"><td class="ln">exhaustion_short (roll-over fade)</td><td class="num">+217</td><td class="num">6</td><td>The 4-claim run +$372.5 fading the top, minus 2 late stops.</td></tr>
</tbody>
</table>
</div>

<div class="callout"><div class="ct"><strong>The two calls that went wrong on Thursday — and why they're worth more than the wins.</strong> The router made two genuinely bad calls this day, both caught and both banked into the desk's rulebook. (1) At <strong>13:36 it benched grind on the US-open flush</strong> — the day gave back a chunk in 90 seconds and the router read grind as churning. You overrode it ("liven grind back up"), grind then rode to new highs and booked +$234/+$197 targets. Lesson banked: <em>don't bench the aligned trend-rider on open stop-outs when the day is cleanly trending — the open is structurally violent, that's noise, only bench if the trend structure actually breaks.</em> (2) At <strong>15:49 it flipped to longs on a delta-blip in ER-0.01 dead chop</strong> — a four-bucket buyer build and a +45-point poke of the range top. It faded in two minutes. You caught it ("why is grind buying into chop"). Lesson banked: <em>re-arm momentum only on a real break with ER actually climbing and vol expanding, never on a delta blip alone.</em> Neither mistake cost real money (both got salvaged to roughly +$55), but the process errors are the ones that write the rules.</div></div>

<div class="card">
<h3>The one call that clearly saved money</h3>
<p>At 14:51, when ER collapsed from 0.40 to 0.07 over two ticks and price rolled off the highs, the router <strong>proactively benched both momentum longs while the desk was flat</strong> — not reacting to stops, reading the regime flip ahead of them. Over the window that followed, the momentum shadows that would have kept firing lost about <strong>−$400</strong> (thrust_aligned −$202, thrust_cont −$118, thrust_loose −$78). The desk sat flat and protected instead. That single defensive read is the clearest example of the router paying for itself: it's cheap to be wrongly benched (you miss a few trades), expensive to be wrongly armed (you churn), and this call landed on the right side of that asymmetry.</p>
</div>

<h2><span class="n">Case 2</span> Wednesday — the clean two-way trend, both sides caught</h2>

<p class="lead">Wednesday (+$638 on just 17 trades) is the "when it's real, take it and go home" day. The tape ran a strong down-leg and then a strong up-leg, and — unusually — the desk caught both directions cleanly.</p>

<div class="card">
<h3>abs_veto_short's day</h3>
<p>The star was <strong>abs_veto_short: 10 trades, +$570.5</strong>, riding the down-leg. It only won 4 of those 10 — but one of them was a <strong>+$536 chandelier runner</strong>, the single best trade of the entire week, and that one tail dwarfed the small stops around it. This is the abs_veto shape in one trade: the 55-second continuation-veto keeps it out of the fake thrusts, and when it does catch a real one, the chandelier lets it run. Then late in the session the tape turned and trended up cleanly; the router benched the shorts (correctly — you don't short a strong up-move) and let the longs ride. Both legs, both green, 17 trades total. That's the ideal day and it's worth remembering it looks nothing like Thursday's 91-trade brawl.</p>
</div>

<h2><span class="n">Case 3</span> Friday — the enable-all-at-open bleed</h2>

<p class="lead">Friday (−$905) is the honest graveyard of the week and the sharpest single lesson in it. The US open printed a real up-thrust (ER 0.68, a clean break to 28713) and the book was set to "all six gates on" for the open. The thrust then fully reversed — a −172-point round-trip, ER collapsing from 0.50 to 0.12 into ATR-62 violence — and the wrong gates were holding the bag.</p>

<div class="card">
<h3>The open, gate by gate (13:30–14:30 UTC)</h3>
<table>
<thead><tr><th>Gate at the open</th><th class="num">Net $</th><th class="num">Stops</th><th>What happened</th></tr></thead>
<tbody>
<tr class="row-hl"><td class="ln">abs_veto_long (veto-protected)</td><td class="num">+146.5</td><td class="num">—</td><td>Caught the real thrust, banked it, then its veto kept it OUT of the reversal.</td></tr>
<tr class="row-bad"><td class="ln">exhaustion_short (off-regime)</td><td class="num">−72.0</td><td class="num">1</td><td>Fired the new 0.5R/1.5R scalp trial into ATR-62 — a scalp can't cover a 62-point stop.</td></tr>
<tr class="row-bad"><td class="ln">grind_long (no veto)</td><td class="num">−284.0</td><td class="num">4</td><td>Bought the rollover top, walled — four stops in ninety seconds.</td></tr>
</tbody>
</table>
<p>The contrast is the whole lesson, and it's now a banked rule: <strong>at a violent open, arm the veto-gates, not the churners.</strong> abs_veto's 55-second confirmation made it +$146 by only taking the thrust that <em>continued</em>; grind, with no such filter, chased the reversal and lost −$284. The rest of the day was a choppy recovery (ER 0.24–0.30 — elevated but not clean), and two attempts to re-arm abs_veto into it churned ≈−$245 more before you called it and went fully flat by 19:08. Going flat was the right end-state: the live shadows confirmed every benched gate would have kept bleeding through the ATR-52/62 chop. Some days the winning move is no move.</p>
</div>

<div class="callout"><div class="ct"><strong>One Friday lead for the shadow desk (Part 2):</strong> the only configuration that made money on Friday's waterfall was <em>rg_long_fast</em> at <strong>+$249</strong>, fading the oversold flush and buying the capitulation dips — while our live long-fader, capitulation_long, bled. That's a flag that the right flush-catcher might be rg_long_fast, not capitulation. Chased down properly in the shadow section.</div></div>

<div class="card">
<h3>The STOP_UNFILLED self-heals</h3>
<p>Six trades this week exited on STOP_UNFILLED — the known issue where IBKR won't fire a resting stop's trigger on the continuous contract. Every one of them was an abs_veto trade, and here's the important part: <strong>the stop-breach guard caught all six and bounded each loss to roughly one ATR.</strong> None ran naked, none turned into a drift-halt. That's the self-heal working as designed.</p>
