<p>So the software failure cost about −$274 this week, all of it bounded and none of it catastrophic. The proper fix — resting stops on the concrete front-month contract instead of the continuous one — is written and sitting on a branch but is <em>not deployed</em>, so this guard is still doing the work. Worth deploying and live-verifying; until then the guard is the safety net and it held six times out of six.</p>
</div>

<h2><span class="n">The router</span> The direction-router trial — how it behaved</h2>

<p class="lead">This was the week the Claude-run router stopped being a session experiment and became durable infrastructure — a headless systemd tick went live Friday that manages the desk even with no chat session running. Across the week the router changed the gate switches <strong>31 times</strong>. It is benching-only, fully reversible, and paper — it can only block new entries, never force a trade — so its worst case is a missed opportunity, not a loss.</p>

<div class="card">
<h3>The router's own scorecard</h3>
<p>Every switch change gets graded in the bad-call ledger once the outcome is known. The running tally sits at <strong>17 GOOD · 2 BAD · 4 CHURN · 2 NEUTRAL</strong>. The honest read: most calls helped or cost nothing, the two outright bad calls both happened on Thursday and both were caught within minutes and turned into permanent rules, and the "churn" entries are process-thrash (net roughly zero in dollars) rather than money errors.</p>
<table>
<thead><tr><th>Grade</th><th class="num">Count</th><th>Meaning &amp; the notable ones</th></tr></thead>
<tbody>
<tr class="row-hl"><td class="ln">✅ GOOD</td><td class="num">17</td><td>Calls that helped or avoided loss — incl. the Thu 14:51 regime-flip bench (≈−$400 churn avoided) and arming the chop-book that produced exhaustion's +$372.5 run.</td></tr>
<tr class="row-bad"><td class="ln">❌ BAD</td><td class="num">2</td><td>Both on Thu: benching the trend-rider on the open flush, and the premature delta-blip flip to longs. Both banked as rules.</td></tr>
<tr><td class="ln">⚠ CHURN</td><td class="num">4</td><td>Over-toggling grind ~8× on Thursday morning; the Sunday-reopen "all six on"; two Friday re-enables into a choppy recovery. Thrashy, ≈$0 net.</td></tr>
<tr><td class="ln">◻ NEUTRAL</td><td class="num">2</td><td>Veto-protected arms that never fired = zero cost, correct mechanism choice.</td></tr>
</tbody>
</table>
</div>

<div class="card">
<h3>What the router got right, in plain terms</h3>
<p>Strip out the drama and the router did its core job well: it kept the counter-trend faders benched almost the entire week (rgv_short fired only 3 times all week and abs_veto_short was correctly held out of the Friday chop after Wednesday's win), and it read the two big regime flips — Thursday's roll-over and Friday's reversal — closely enough to sit flat through the worst churn. Both green days (Wed +$638, Thu +$1,172) happened <em>under</em> the router. Its recurring weakness is the same one all week and in the ledger: <strong>over-toggling grind tick-to-tick</strong> and re-arming momentum a beat too early on elevated-but-not-clean chop. The fix is discipline, not code — arm grind once on a genuinely strong trend, leave it, and wait for ER to actually climb before believing a break.</p>
</div>

<div class="callout"><div class="ct"><strong>Honest caveats — read before you trust any of this.</strong> This is <strong>one week of in-sample paper trading</strong>, and a lopsided one: two-thirds of the week's +$625 net came from a single best-ever day, and a big chunk of the green (+$1,223 across 12 trades) was booked by your own manual claims, not by the exits firing on their own — so the automated system alone did less than the headline. The per-gate reads are built on thin counts (rgv_short fired 3 times; exhaustion's new scalp trial only 11), so treat "abs_veto_short is a down-leg catcher" as a one-week observation, not a proven edge — it rests heavily on a single +$536 runner. The router's scorecard is self-graded. And capitulation_long (−$139) and the new exhaustion 0.5R/1.5R scalp trial (−$48.5, fired off-regime into Friday's violence) both go into Part 1.5's rehab bench rather than getting a face-value bench here — the honest "how could this work" comes next.</div></div>
